    REFACTORING CODE 
This is making it cleaner, faster or more idiomatic without changing its functionality.
This is an area that AI assistants shine, since they are trained in vast amounts of code.
However, to tap into the knowledge effectively, your promot must clarify what "better" means for your situation.
Here's how prompt for refactoring tasks:

    State your refactoring goals explicitly
        Example
        "Refactor the following function to improve its readability and maintainability (reduce repition, use clearer variable names)
        "Optimize this algorithim for speed- It's slow on large inputs"
        By stating specific goals, you help the model decide which transformations to apply.
        If you have multiple goals ensure you list them out all.
        Remember, it will not inherently know what you consider a problem in the code- you must tell it.
    
    Provide the necessary code context 
        Example 
        "I have a React component written as a class. Please refactor it to a functional component using hooks."
        Include version or enviroment details if relevant.
        It's important to include the full function or section that you want to be refactored, and sometimes a bit of surrounding context if relevant.

    Encourage explanations along with the code 
        A great way to learn from an AI-led refactor (and verify its correctness) is to ask for an explanation of the changes.
        Example
        "Please suggest a refactored version of the code, and explain the improvements you made"
        In-short use the AI's ability to explain as a safeguard.

    Use role play to set a high standard 
        For refactoring you might say 
        "Act as a seasoned Typescript expert and refactor the code to align with the best practices and modern standards"
        "Act like an experienced Python developer mentoring a junior. Provide explanations and write docstrings. rewrite the code to optimize it"
        This often yields not just superficial changes, but more insightful improvements because the AI tries to live up to the expert persona.


Now lets walk through an example of refactoring to see how prompt can influence the outcome.
We will use a scenario on JavaScript (Node.js) where we have some less-than-ideal code and we want it improved.

    REFACTORING EXAMPLE: POOR VS IMPROVED PROMPT 

    Suppose you have a function that makes two database calls and does some processing.
    It works, but it's not pretty - there's duplicated code and it's hard to read.
    We wil like to refactor it for clarity and efficiency.
    Here is the original code:

        // Original function: Fetches two lists and processes them (needs refactoring)
        async function getCombinedData(apiClient) {
        // Fetch list of users
        const usersResponse = await apiClient.fetch('/users');
        if (!usersResponse.ok) {
            throw new Error('Failed to fetch users');
        }
        const users = await usersResponse.json();
        // Fetch list of orders
        const ordersResponse = await apiClient.fetch('/orders');
        if (!ordersResponse.ok) {
            throw new Error('Failed to fetch orders');
        }
        const orders = await ordersResponse.json();
        // Combine data (match user to orders)
        const result = [];
        for (let user of users) {
            const userOrders = orders.filter(o => o.userId === user.id);
            result.push({ user, orders: userOrders });
        }
        return result;
        }

    Issues in the code:
        We see duplicated fetch logic for users and orders (could be refactored into a helper or loop), Lack of error detail (Just a generic error message), and it could perhaps run the fetches in parallel to improved speed.
    Lets see two prompting approaches:

        Prompt 1 Underspecified
            "Refactor the above getCombinedData function"
        AI response:
          async function getCombinedData(apiClient) {
            // Fetch users and orders in parallel
            const [usersResponse, ordersResponse] = await Promise.all([
                apiClient.fetch('/users'),
                apiClient.fetch('/orders')
            ]);
            if (!usersResponse.ok || !ordersResponse.ok) {
                throw new Error('Failed to fetch data');
            }
            const [users, orders] = await Promise.all([
                usersResponse.json(),
                ordersResponse.json()
            ]);
            // Map orders by userId for efficient lookup
            const ordersByUser = {};
            for (const order of orders) {
                if (!ordersByUser[order.userId]) {
                ordersByUser[order.userId] = [];
                }
                ordersByUser[order.userId].push(order);
            }
            // Combine data
            return users.map(user => ({
                user,
                orders: ordersByUser[user.id] || []
            }));
            }  

        In this case the AI happened to make good choices, but it assumed a lot because the prompt was open ended.
        It assumed that performance should be improved (parallel request, which is a smart change but what if parallel wasn't safe here?), and it changed error handling to a generic message "Failed to fetch data"
        This illustrates that an underspecified prompt can sometimes get good results on simple cases, but for complex code, it might go off in an undesired direction or miss certain improvements you care about.

    
        Prompt 2 Goal oriented 
            Refactor the above getCombinedData function to eliminate duplicate code and improve performance.
            Specially;
                Avoid repeating the fetch logic for users and orders- maybe use a helper or fetch them together.
                Fetch both lists in parallel if possible
                Keep the error handling for each fetch (we want to know which call failed)
                Improve the combination of data, possibly by using a more efficien structure for lookup instead of a nested loop.
                Provide the refactored code with comments explaining the changes.

            AI's response 
                async function getCombinedData(apiClient) {
                // 1. Fetch users and orders in parallel to improve performance
                const [usersResponse, ordersResponse] = await Promise.all([
                    apiClient.fetch('/users'),
                    apiClient.fetch('/orders')
                ]);

                // 2. Handle errors separately to know which call failed
                if (!usersResponse.ok) {
                    throw new Error('Failed to fetch users');
                }
                if (!ordersResponse.ok) {
                    throw new Error('Failed to fetch orders');
                }

                // Parse JSON responses in parallel
                const [users, orders] = await Promise.all([
                    usersResponse.json(),
                    ordersResponse.json()
                ]);

                // 3. Use a lookup map for orders by userId for efficient matching
                const ordersByUser = orders.reduce((map, order) => {
                    if (!map[order.userId]) {
                    map[order.userId] = [];
                    }
                    map[order.userId].push(order);
                    return map;
                }, {});

                // 4. Combine data using the lookup map
                return users.map(user => ({
                    user,
                    orders: ordersByUser[user.id] || []  // empty array if no orders for user
                }));
                }

            Analysis
            This response directly addressed all the specified goals.
            The code is refactored to be cleaner and faster, and it maintained seperate error messages such as requested.
            This is a great outcome because we, as the prompter, defined what "refactor" meant in the context.
            we guided the AI to produce a solution that matches our needs with minimal back-and-forth.

When you know what you want improved, spell it out. 
AI is good at following instructions, but it won't read your mind.

Additional refactoring tips include;
    Refactor in steps 
    Ask for alternative approaches
    Combine refactoring with explanation to learn patterns
    Validation and testing 
    

        